## Dispatch-time stack-caching of accessed resources

The dispatch codegen caches the resources a fiber accesses into a
stack-local array before entering the morsel loop, then runs the loop
reading from the stack copies. LLVM proves the stack array does not
alias the column buffers and promotes the cached resources to
registers, so the hot loop does zero resource memory loads. This is a
property of the emitted per-fiber dispatch function (HILA-RUNTIME-C2,
the dispatch-codegen rust-pipe pattern), not a standalone type. An
earlier placeholder conflated this optimization with the phase-boundary
ResourceSnapshot view; the two are unrelated, and the stack-caching
belongs to codegen.

## Unified columnar storage migration

The api ships the ColumnStorage contract and the Decompose mechanism
(the unified-storage contract round); the engine then unwinds its
disjoint bespoke stores onto that one contract. These are deferred
follow-on shapes, recorded here so the framing is honest about the
target state while the current code still carries the shipped arenas.

The resource data plane (the resource bindings, BindingsFor / DrainStores)
collapses onto ColumnStorage: a resource is a decomposed set of scalar
columns, so it stops being a separate mechanism. The execution plan
already flattened the dense nested const-array tree into CSR-style flat
pools (a phases array, a flat trunks pool, a flat fibers pool sized by
the plan-wide caps), which dissolved the multi-megabyte ExecutionPlan
value structurally and made compute_execution_plan usable at the real
default budget (the value no longer overflows the stack). The
store-backing step moves those flat columns onto ColumnStorage, each
reserved by live count, turning ExecutionPlan into a handle over the
columns: a store-backed meta-resource. The adapt metrics arena and the
dispatch slot buffers route through the store the same way.

The naive arena-backed ColumnStorage placeholder impl ships in
hilavitkutin-providers (the crate already reserved for future
ColumnStorage / Clock / Memory defaults). A storage lint forbids any
runtime-data storage outside the contract (no hand-rolled arena, no
MaybeUninit pool, no raw fixed buffer); the thread-pool PoolFrame is
the one sanctioned fixed-array exception. Each store migration is its
own round on the unified-storage arc; the topological-dispatch slice
reworks on top (its topo order becomes a store-backed meta-resource).

## GATE-2 const grouping and per-trunk dispatch

The GATE-2 parallel engine assigns each work unit to a phase and a
trunk, computed at compile time from the registered bundle. Each unit's
read and write access sets fold to store-bit masks over the global store
numbering (the existing const MaskProject fold); read-after-write edges
between masks form the unit dependency DAG, whose waist-delimited sections
(concurrency minima, via arvo's const waist detection) are the phases, so a
producer to consumer chain with no interior narrowing is one phase. Within a
phase the column-disjoint components are the trunks. A trunk's units share no write column with
another trunk in the same phase, so trunks run on separate cores with no
cross-trunk synchronisation, joined only by the waist barrier between
phases.

The grouping drives a const-gated dispatch walk: a flat walk over the
work-unit carrier where each position is gated by a compile-time check of
its phase and trunk, so dead-code elimination collapses the walk into one
member-only program per phase and trunk. Run one such program per core.
This is the codegen flattener of the canonical design realised in pure
Rust through const evaluation and dead-code elimination, with no
proc-macro, build script, or compiler pass. It supersedes the earlier
value-carrying nested carrier walk for trunk isolation; the per-fiber
RunFiber walk is reused unchanged as the innermost member execution.

This lands across the GATE-2 build rounds: the const grouping
computation first, then the const-gated single-core dispatch that
re-points the scheduler run path, then core-pinning the trunks onto the
thread pool. The shipped DESIGN gains the trunk, phase, and waist model
as the actual run path when the dispatch re-point lands and the behavior
becomes observable.

Round 2a lands the const-gated walk machinery: a fold of the value
carrier into per-unit masks, a per-unit run-head step on the fiber
walk, a gated trait that runs only the members of one phase and trunk
(every other position folds away), and a phase-count helper. This is
the machinery that turns the compile-time grouping into one member-only
program per phase and trunk. The const-generic phase and trunk
dispatcher, and re-pointing the scheduler run path through it, follow
in round 2b.

Round 2b re-points the scheduler run path to dispatch phase by phase:
each phase's units run fully before the next, the waist sectioning, with
the phase membership taken from the round-1 grouping and the existing
gated walk running each phase's units. Single-core this is sequential
with degenerate (no-op) barriers between phases, output identical to the
flat walk; G2-N replaces the degenerate barrier with the real phase
barrier and pins each phase's trunks to cores.

The change_seen_count metric (the change_class adapt-axis numerator) is
maintained in the single-core run fold only. The run_parallel and
run_fused folds reset store_dirty without counting it, so a ScheduleEnd
hook reading change_seen_count under those paths reads stuck-at-zero
beside the live pass_count, last_record_count, and pass-duration EMA.
Mirroring the single-core capture-and-increment into both folds, plus a
parallel/fused test, is the follow-up.

## ASM-emission contract tests (generalized typestate codegen assertions)

The `asm_gate` binary (`mock/benches/asm_gate_fixtures/` plus `cargo run --bin
asm_gate`) disassembles four `#[no_mangle]` fixtures and asserts zero indirect
calls. That catches devirtualisation regression but only that single property on
four fixed fixtures. The generalization: a standing per-typestate ASM-emission
contract harness. For each codegen-load-bearing typestate shape, a fixture exports
a stable named symbol; the harness disassembles it through the existing
nm/objdump/otool fallback chain, scopes to that symbol's region in the (large)
dump, and asserts the instructions expected are present and the ones not wanted are
absent. Assertions are properties, not fragile exact-instruction matches: no
indirect call, no call to a named runtime helper, a specific inlined op present, a
fused store present. A typestate change that regresses codegen then fails the test
with the reason, where the perf bench would only surface the symptom. Each new
typestate-bearing slice (per-fiber morsel windowing, RCM-row dispatch, head+tail
convergence, phase-overlap progress counters) adds its fixture and its
present/absent assertions. Rides alongside the perf gate; both are standing red
oracles until the codegen matches. Discipline detail in roadmap research
202606201900.
